  "response": "Here's a Python function that formats a number as currency with a `$` sign, commas separating every three digits, and a minus sign placed before the dollar sign for negative values:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"Format a number as a currency string with $ and thousand separators.\"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return f\"-${abs(n):,}\"\n    else:\n        return f\"${n:,}\"\n```\n\nThis function:\n- Wraps the absolute value of the number in commas.\n- Adds the `$` sign at the front.\n- Places the minus sign before the `$` for negative numbers.\n\nExample usage:\n\n```python\nprint(format_currency(4640039))     # $4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(-4640039))    # -$4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(0))           # $0\nprint(format_currency(123))         # $123\nprint(format_currency(123456789))   # $123,456,789\n```\n\nNote: The format specifier `{:,.}` adds commas as thousand separators. Using this with negative numbers requires the absolute value because Python's built-in formatting does not automatically place a minus sign when using the `,` format specifier. This function ensures correct behavior for both positive and negative inputs.",
